      "text": "The math books must stay together, so we can treat them as a single \"super book\". Similarly, the English books must stay together, so we can treat them as a single \"super book\". This gives us a total of 2 \"books\" to arrange on the shelf: one \"super book\" of math books and one \"super book\" of English books.\nThe number of ways to arrange the 2 \"books\" on the shelf is $2!$, which is equal to 2.\nWithin the \"super book\" of math books, there are $3!$ ways to arrange the 3 math books.\nWithin the \"super book\" of English books, there are $5!$ ways to arrange the 5 English books.\nTherefore, the total number of ways to arrange the books on the shelf is $2! \\cdot 3! \\cdot 5! = 2 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 120 = \\boxed{1440}$.\nThe answer is: 1440",
