      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the problem of determining the number of ways to arrange 3 math books and 5 English books on a shelf such that all the math books stay together and all the English books stay together, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Treat the Math Books as One Unit**: Since all the math books must stay together, we can treat them as a single unit. Similarly, since all the English books must stay together, we can treat them as another single unit.\n\n2. **Identify the Units**: We have two units to arrange: one unit of math books and one unit of English books.\n\n3. **Permutations of Units**: The number of ways to arrange these two units (one unit of math books and one unit of English books) is given by the factorial of the number of units. Since there are 2 units, the number of ways to arrange them is \\(2!\\):\n   \\[\n   2! = 2\n   \\]\n\n4. **Arrange the Books Within Each Unit**: \n   - The 3 math books can be arranged among themselves in \\(3!\\) ways:\n     \\[\n     3! = 6\n     \\]\n   - The 5 English books can be arranged among themselves in \\(5!\\) ways:\n     \\[\n     5! = 120\n     \\]\n\n5. **Combine the Arrangements**: To find the total number of ways to arrange the 8 books (3 math books and 5 English books), we multiply the number of ways to arrange the units by the number of ways to arrange the books within each unit:\n   \\[\n   2! \\times 3! \\times 5! = 2 \\times 6 \\times 120\n   \\]\n\n6. **Calculate the Final Answer**:\n   \\[\n   2 \\times 6 \\times 120 = 1440\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of ways to arrange the 3 math books and 5 English books on the shelf, with all math books together and all English books together, is \\(\\boxed{1440}\\).",
