    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the problem of arranging 3 math books and 5 English books on a shelf such that all math books and all English books are grouped together, we can break the problem down into several steps.\n\n1. **Treat the math books as a single unit and the English books as a single unit:**\n   - Since all the math books must stay together, we can consider the math books as a single \"super book.\"\n   - Similarly, since all the English books must stay together, we can consider the English books as another \"super book.\"\n   - This means we have 2 units to arrange: one unit of math books and one unit of English books.\n\n2. **Arrange the 2 units:**\n   - The number of ways to arrange 2 units is given by the factorial of 2, which is \\(2!\\).\n   \\[\n   2! = 2 \\times 1 = 2\n   \\]\n   - So, there are 2 ways to arrange the 2 units (Math Unit, English Unit or English Unit, Math Unit).\n\n3. **Arrange the books within each unit:**\n   - Within the unit of math books, there are 3! ways to arrange the 3 math books.\n   \\[\n   3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n   \\]\n   - Within the unit of English books, there are 5! ways to arrange the 5 English books.\n   \\[\n   5! = 5 \\times 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 120\n   \\]\n\n4. **Combine the arrangements:**\n   - The total number of ways to arrange the 8 books (3 math books and 5 English books) on the shelf, given that all math books are together and all English books are together, is the product of the number of ways to arrange the units and the number of ways to arrange the books within each unit.\n   \\[\n   2! \\times 3! \\times 5! = 2 \\times 6 \\times 120 = 1440\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of ways to arrange the books is \\(\\boxed{1440}\\).",
