      "text": "To solve the problem of arranging 3 math books and 5 English books on a shelf such that all the math books stay together and all the English books stay together, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Treat the math books as a single unit and the English books as another single unit:**\n   Since all the math books must stay together and all the English books must stay together, we can think of the math books as one \"super book\" and the English books as another \"super book.\" This reduces the problem to arranging 2 units on the shelf.\n\n2. **Calculate the number of ways to arrange these 2 units:**\n   The number of ways to arrange 2 distinct units is given by the factorial of 2, which is \\(2!\\):\n   \\[\n   2! = 2 \\times 1 = 2\n   \\]\n   So, there are 2 ways to arrange the 2 units on the shelf.\n\n3. **Calculate the number of ways to arrange the books within each unit:**\n   - The number of ways to arrange the 3 math books is \\(3!\\):\n     \\[\n     3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n     \\]\n   - The number of ways to arrange the 5 English books is \\(5!\\):\n     \\[\n     5! = 5 \\times 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 120\n     \\]\n\n4. **Combine the arrangements:**\n   Since the 2 units can be arranged in 2 ways and within each unit there are specific arrangements, the total number of ways to arrange the books is the product of the number of ways to arrange the 2 units and the number of ways to arrange the books within each unit:\n   \\[\n   2 \\times 3! \\times 5! = 2 \\times 6 \\times 120 = 1440\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of ways to arrange the 3 math books and 5 English books on the shelf is \\(\\boxed{1440}\\).",
